# Signup

Sign the user up for an event in the OpenClaw browser, using only the details in
the **Profile** section at the bottom of this file. A bare event link with no other
message means "sign me up for this".

## Rules

- **Only facts from the Profile.** Every value you enter comes from the Profile.
  Never invent or guess a job title, employer, project, city, phone number or any
  other fact about the user. If a required field cannot be answered from the
  Profile, do not guess: hand off (step 6) and name the field.
- **Copy contact details verbatim**, exactly as the Profile writes them.
- **Write like a person.** Free-text answers are short and plain. Use hyphens,
  never em-dashes, in anything you type into a form or send to the user.
- **Never** create an account, type a password, enter an email or SMS code, or
  enter payment details. If a ticket costs money, hand off.
- **Do not opt in** to newsletters, marketing or optional extras. Tick only the
  boxes the form requires (code of conduct, privacy, terms).
- **One event at a time.** There is ONE shared browser. If several links arrive
  together, finish one (registered or handed off) before opening the next. Never
  fill two forms in the same turn - it wedges the browser (stale refs, timeouts,
  failed turns).

## Procedure

1. **Check it is a signup page.** A link counts only if it is an event,
   registration, waitlist, RSVP or application page. A link to anything else
   (an article, a repo) is not a signup. If unclear, ask one short question
   before doing anything.

2. **Open it in the managed `openclaw` browser profile** (the default). Wait a
   few seconds for any page-load bot check to clear before interacting. If the
   site shows the user as signed out where a sign-in would normally exist (for
   example Luma), say so and hand off - do not try to sign in.

3. **Fill the form** from the Profile. Per field type:
   - **Text / textarea:** fill the whole value in one action and check it landed
     complete (typing long text key by key can drop characters).
   - **Native `<select>`:** select the exact option label.
   - **Searchable dropdown** (typing filters the list): type to filter, click the
     option, check the field shows it.
   - **Custom dropdown** (fixed option panel): open it, take a snapshot, click the
     option, click an empty area to close the panel (an open panel hijacks the
     next click), then check the field shows your value. One dropdown at a time.
   - **Phone with a country selector:** set the country code first, then the number.
   - **Required consent boxes:** tick them. For dietary needs pick "None" unless
     the Profile says otherwise.
   - **"What are you building / working on?"** paste the Profile's answer for it
     character for character. Do not shorten, extend or reword it.
   - **Other free text** (about you, why this event, expectations): one to three
     sentences built only from the Profile, fitted to this event.
   - **Optional fields** the Profile does not cover: leave empty.

4. **Submit** - at most 2 attempts, no hammer-retry.

5. **On success** (a confirmation, "pending approval" or "on the waitlist" state):
   reply with the event title, date, and which state it is in. Done.

6. **On any blocker, hand off.** A blocker is a captcha or "verify you are human"
   step (Cloudflare Turnstile, reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha), a sign-in wall, an email or
   SMS code, a payment step, or a required field the Profile cannot answer. Do not
   close the tab.

   a. **Queue it for `/signup`**, which pre-fills it again in the user's own
      Chrome through Claude Code:

      ```bash
      bash {baseDir}/scripts/handoff.sh \
        --url "<event url>" --title "<event title>" \
        --ticket "<ticket or button label - leave this flag out if none>" \
        --prefill '<JSON object: form field label -> the value you filled>' \
        --needs-user "<what is left for the user, comma-separated>"
      ```

      It prints a receipt with an `id:` line. Never say "queued" without that
      id, and never write the queue file yourself. If the command fails or you
      are not allowed to run it, carry on with the reply and say why.

   b. **Reply** in this shape:

      ```
      Couldn't finish: <event title>
      <event url>
      Blocked by: <what stopped you>
      Filled in so far:
      - <field>: <value>
      - ...
      Left for you: <each remaining field>, then submit.
      Queued for /signup: <id from the receipt>
      ```

      If it was not queued, write `Not queued: <why>` in place of the last line.

7. **Ambiguous outcome** (you cannot positively see a success state): treat it as
   a blocker (step 6) and say you could not confirm it. Never report "registered"
   without seeing it on the page.

